## 7722 title='Page 64'
   transc: 4.30 P. M. Cincinnati Jany 24th 1865 Cincinnati Jany 24th 3 P. M. for Hon C. A. Dana Washn shall get off sixteen thousand by midnight then will be about three or four thousand who will go tomorrow I shall be at Columbus tomorrow Wheeling next day ---- ---- Lewis B. Parsons Col &c 6 PM Norfolk Jan 24 1865. Norfolk Sec Navy The Saugus left this morning for Washn ---- Gen Grant needs her at once ---- she must be now half way ---- no use for me to send for her ---- will you let a message <insertion>meet</insertion> her & turn her back J. M Berrin Comdt 420 PM Beckwith City Pt Winchester Va Jan 24. 1865. 7 PM 24th Gen Grant ---- I wish to fit up a canvas pontoon bridge for the cavalry of this mily Divn. & want a co. of pontooners to take charge of it ---- will you have the kindness to order a co. to me from the Army of the Potomac I should like to have the same co. & officer that I had with me on my Expedition in June last to Trevillian Station. PH Sheridan MGC
## 7741 title='Page 83'
   transc: 2.15 PM Fort Monroe Va Feby 2nd 1865 1. P.M. For Secy of Washn, Steamer George. leary just arrived from Savannah. Major Anderson bearer of dispatches from Gen Sherman to Gen Schofield leaves for Annapolis on mail boat Webster this P M No news of importance beyond dispatches. Capt Hudson of Gen Grants Staff also arrived by same boat with detailed dispatch for Gen Grant J. J. Eckert 3. P. M Ft Monroe Va Feby 2nd 1865 2.30 P. M for Bates, ascertain enemy by. what boat the President left Annapolis & the time of leaving and telegraph me in cipher also the time the boat passes Pt Lookout Eckert 730 P. M Ft Monroe Feby 2nd 1865 7. P M. For Fred'k W. Seward Asst Secy State Richmond party are here. President not arrived Wm. H. Seward another to Secy War. Richmond party here. President not arrived I do not recognize them until he Comes Wm H. Seward Secy State
## 8561 title='Page 83'
   transc: 4 P. M. Ft Monroe Feby 2nd 1865 4 P. M. for Gen Schofield Washn or Annapolis . I arrived here today with despatches for you from Gen Sherman will be in Annapolis tomorrow morning (Friday) sig John F Anderson Maj & A. D. C. 710 pm 2d Ft Monroe Feb 2 1865 Capt Blodgett a Q m Annapolis ---- Please inform Gen Schofield should he arrive in annapolis tonight that I will be at Annapolis to morrow Fryday morning with important dispatches for him from Gen Sherman J. F Anderson Major & A. D. C. 7 PM 8 pm Annapolis Feb 2 1865 Gen D. H. Rucker Telegram recd . The steamer Ariel can take one thousand men & 60 officers The New York can take 1500 . To put more on would crowd them very much . Gen Meagher requests that steamers which have reported be directed to take his command G. S. Blodgett Capt. A Q M
## 7787 title='Page 129'
   transc: for McCaine, 730 P. M. 8.30 P. M. Winchester, City Pt Va Feby 26, 1865. Gen Sheridan ---- Shermans movements will depend on the amount of op = position he meets from the Enemy, if strongly opposed he may possibly have to fall back to Georgetown S. C. & fit out for a new start ---- I think however all danger of the necessity for going to that point has passed I believe he has passed Charlotte he will now aim to connect with Schofield about Goldsboro he may take Fayetteville in his way to Goldsboro ---- if you reach Lynchburg you will have to be guided in your after movements by the information you obtain ---- before you could possibly reach Sherman I think you would find him moving from Goldsboro towards Raleigh or engaging the Enemy strongly posted at one or the other of these places with RR communications opened from his army to Wilmington or Newberne US Grant Lt Gen 945 PM Ft Monroe Feb 25 1865 Norfolk twenty fifth to Hon. E. B. Washburn Chairman Committee of Commerce Washn ---- I sent for Johnson to try him ---- It would be a pity that the greatest rascal should escape ---- I can send him to you under guard but do not let him turn states evidence ---- His confidential is clear ---- Do you want him sig George H Gordon Br Gen
## 4788 title='Page 347'
   transc: 445 pm Fort Monroe July 10 1864 Ft Monroe 10th 15 am July 10th Gen Halleck - none of the New Orleans troops have arrived yet J W Shaffer Col and Chief of staff 5 5 pm Balto July 10th 1864 Baltimore 10th 5 pm for Secy of War The undersigned have good reason to believe that an effort will be made to release the seventeen thousand confederate Prisoners that are now at Point Lookout . we beg leave to call your attention to it B F Camp and R Stockett Mathews 535 pm Clarksburg July 10th 1864 Genl Sullivan occupied Hedgeville last night and is moving east today he thinks rebels strength ten thousand but citizens say twelve or fifteen thousand thats latest can obtain CO Rowe
## 7802 title='Page 144'
   transc: 11 AM City Point Mch 5th 1865 1030 am 5th. Gen Halleck. I have not heard <insertion>whether</insertion> it was still intended to send an expedition from Washn to break up the Orange an Alexandria road ---- an expedition over two thousand strong left here to go up the Rappahannock to break the trade carried on across the northern neck. they will go to Fredericksburg first - US Grant 1145 AM City Point Mch 5th 1865 11 AM 5th to Secretary of War Deserters in this morning report that Sheridan had routed Early and captured Charlottsville They report four regiments having gone from here to reenforce Early US Grant Lt Gen 2.20 P. M Ft Monroe Va Mar 5th 65 2.30 P. M. for Secy of Navy Washn. The monitor Sangamon is now in & is on her way up the James River sig O. S. Glisson Capt &c
## 8486 title='Page 8'
   transc: 1 PM. 28. Ft. Monroe Dec. 28. 1864 Ft Mon Dec 27th for C. C. Fulton Balto. ---- arrived here this Evening from off Wilmington have sent a dispatch to G. V. Fox which if approved you will get I shall start immedy on dispatch Stmr. Signed A Fulton ---- Another Ft Monroe twenty seventh for G. V. Fox Asst. Sec Navy Wash ---- just arrived here on Stmr. Santiago - de - Cuba from off Wilmington ---- attack on fort Fisher commenced 12 noon Saturday twenty fourth continuing till night , Resumed Sunday & kept up with great vigor through out day , fort much damaged all barracks & store houses burnt garrison driven to bomb proofs scarcely venturing to reply , small portion troops landed on Sunday afternoon , skirmishes with great gallantry pushed up to the fort & actually penetrated works , killed a bearer of dispatches whilst Entering the fort & Lt Wallen of one hundred forty second N. Y. Regt captured rebel flag from the outer bastion ---- Troops also captured a whole battalion rebel troops outside works , but strange to say our troops were with drawn & ordered to reembark ---- Adm Porter however continues the attack & is confident of success ---- on Sunday 3 boats crews sent by Capt Glisson captured flag Pond Hill battery with Sixty five men who are now on board Santiago - the powderboat explosion was successfully made result not ascertained but thought to have done some damage ---- if approved please let this go to Balt American
## 7818 title='Page 160'
   transc: 3.40 P. M City Pt Mar 10th 1865 11 30 AM 10th, For Secy of War, ---- I would respectfully recommend that Brevet Br Gen T. M. Harris be appointed full Br Genl of Vols. Most of his regiment goes out of service in the next few weeks, two company of it in the next two days and he is the only officer in his Division to command it ---- US Grant Lt Genl 130 PM 2 pm Ft. Monroe Va Mar 12. 1865 Sec. Navy. ---- Mahopac is in from Charleston & the Cosco from N. Y. & both are on their way up the James river ---- we have no news of the Montauk ---- O. S. Glisson Capt 140PM City Point Mch 13th 1865 1 pm 13th for Secy of War ---- I am in receipt of a letter of the 7th from Schofield ---- at that time Cox was within three miles of Kinston and repairs on the RR were going on rapidly. Hokes Div was confronting, Schofield was going out himself and expected to push out and take Kinston at once Palmer was ordered and should have taken ---- Kinston whilst Hodges was at Wilmington I have not yet learned his excuse for his failure US Grant JA Rawlins Lt Genl Br Gen &c
## 8622 title='Page 144'
   transc: 1030 Am Ft Monroe Mch 5. 1865 Ft Monroe 10 am for Secy Navy Washn . Your telegram was received this morning at 15 minutes after midnight . Blowing a gale of wind at the time US steamer Ariel sailed at day - light this morning . The monitors are expected every moment from Cape fear and I shall send them up the river immediately O S. Glisson Capt Senior Officer 2.50 P. M City Pt 5th 1865 2. P. M. 5th Secy of War . Deserters from every point of the Enemys line confirm the capture of Charlottsville of Gen Sheridan they say he captured Early and nearly his entire forces consisting of eighteen hundred men . four brigades are reported as being sent to Lynchburg to get there before Gen Sheridan If possible . I think there is no doubt but Gen Sheridan will at least succeed in destroying the James river Canal U. S. Grant Lt Genl
